            <ArticleTitle>Retractile mesenteritis: report of a case.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>547-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Retractile mesenteritis is a rare idiopathic inflammatory process of the mesenteric adipose tissue. A 58-year-old woman who presented with acute abdominal pain and vomiting underwent laparotomy for a small bowel obstruction of unknown etiology. The laparotomy showed dilated small bowel loops and multiple tumor-like fibrous plaques in the mesentery. Histological examination revealed nonspecific chronic inflammatory processes of the mesentery. The computed tomography finding of increased density of the mesenteric fat, known as the &quot;fat-ring sign,&quot; in a patient with small bowel obstruction and no surgical history would suggest this rare diagnosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
